explain in detail the computation, formula, algorithm used to do the AC circuit analysis in ACCircuit.h and ACCircuit.cpp

When runFrequencySweepAnalysis runs, it executes the following logic loop:

Initialize Frequency = startFreq
While Frequency <= endFreq:
    1. Calculate Period T = 1.0 / Frequency
    2. Set Time Step dt = T / 4.0
    
    3. [Step 1: Peak State Solution]
       - Set current time = dt
       - Clear G and B matrix containers
       - Call stamp() on all components using dt and current time
       - Solve G * statePeak = B using LU Decomposition
       
    4. [Step 2: Quadrature State Solution]
       - Set current time = 2.0 * dt
       - Clear G and B matrix containers
       - Pass statePeak as the historical 'oldV' context vector
       - Call stamp() on all components using dt and current time
       - Solve G * stateQuarter = B using LU Decomposition
       
    5. [Polar Extraction]
       - For each tracked Node/Branch:
         Real = statePeak[i], Imag = stateQuarter[i]
         Calculate Magnitude, Phase, and Decibels (dB)
         
    6. [Output & Log]
       - Print row values to the terminal table
       - Stream comma-separated metrics to bode_plot_data.csv
       
    7. [Logarithmic Increment]
       - Frequency = Frequency * 10^(1.0 / pointsPerDecade)



explain in detail the computation, formula, algorithm used to do the DC circuit analysis in DCCircuit.h and DCCircuit.cpp

The DC Sweep systematically maps out circuit performance across an input span by placing the MNA solver loop inside a linear step parameter framework.

Initialize: V1_value = startV (0.0V)
While V1_value <= endV (12.0V):
   1. sweepSource->setDCValue(V1_value)
   2. Invoke solveMna(currentV, sweepV)
      └─ Runs the entire Newton-Raphson loop to settle the circuit matrices
   3. Write results out to text file row streams:
      • dc_sweep_voltage.csv <- Write node values from sweepV[0] to sweepV[numNodes-1]
      • dc_sweep_current.csv <- Compute and write currents for all elements
      • dc_sweep_power.csv   <- Compute and write powers for all elements
   4. Seed next guess step: currentV = sweepV  (Feed-forward optimization)
   5. Step Value: V1_value += stepV (0.5V)


explain in detail the computation, formula, algorithm used to do the PULSE circuit analysis in PulseCircuit.h and PulseCircuit.cpp

The complete algorithm inside PulseCircuit::runPulseTransientAnalysis combines these modules into an iterative process:

Initialize: time = 0.0s, oldV = [0.0, 0.0, ...]
While time <= tEnd (durationMicroseconds):

    1. Initialize Newton-Raphson Guess for this time-step: nrGuessV = oldV
    2. Newton-Raphson Iteration Loop (Max 40 times):
       a. Clear structural matrices G and B to 0.0
       b. Loop through ALL components:
          - VoltageSource: reads current time, evaluates PULSE logic, stamps G and B
          - Resistor: stamps static conductances into G
          - Capacitor: calculates Ieq using terminal values from oldV, stamps G and B
          - Inductor: calculates Veq using current index value from oldV, stamps G and B
          - Diode/BJT (if present): linearizes using values from nrGuessV, stamps G and B
       c. Solve the matrix system: G * nrSolV = B (using math layer LU solver)
       d. Calculate the convergence delta check: maxDelta = max(|nrSolV[i] - nrGuessV[i]|)
       e. Update guess vector for next iteration: nrGuessV = nrSolV
       f. If maxDelta < nrTolerance (1e-6), convergence is reached! Break out of NR loop.
       
    3. Update tracking state configurations:
       - Save capacitor voltage for derivative calculation: prevVcVal = currentV[Node2]
       - Assign completed converged timestep: currentV = nrSolV
       
    4. Post-Process Data and Log:
       - Calculate approximate capacitor current: iC = C * (currentV[Node2] - prevVcVal) / dt
       - Extract inductor current directly from branch current index: iL = currentV[k]
       - Print row values cleanly formatted to the output terminal
       
    5. Advance Timeline: oldV = currentV, time += dt








